  "21": {
    "description": "A1. Month 3. Food and meals — describing what people eat and when. Simple 'would like' for polite requests. Cooking verbs (cook, boil, fry). Vocabulary around 675 words. A simple scene at a table or in a kitchen.",
    "example": "In our house, we eat three meals every day. For breakfast, I eat eggs and bread. I drink tea with milk. My sister eats fruit and yogurt. My brother eats only cereal. For lunch, we eat at school. I usually eat rice or pasta. For dinner, my mother cooks at home. She is a very good cook. She often boils vegetables. She fries fish on Fridays. Tonight she is making soup. Would you like some soup? Yes, please. I like soup a lot. Would you like bread with the soup? Yes, please. Thank you. The soup is hot and tasty. After dinner, we have fruit. Then we wash the dishes together. The kitchen is warm and full of good smells.",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "Who is responsible for preparing the evening meal in the speaker's household?",
      "options": ["A) The speaker alone, every night", "B) The speaker's mother", "C) Both the speaker and the mother together", "D) The person being offered soup"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    "example2": "Long ago, people did not have stoves or ovens. They cooked their food over an open fire outside the house. They built the fire from wood. They put a heavy black pot over the fire. They boiled water in the pot. They cooked soup, beans, and rice. They fried meat on hot stones. Cooking was slow work. It took many hours. People sat near the fire and talked. The smoke was strong. The food often tasted of smoke too. Today we have many easy ways to cook. We have stoves and ovens and small machines. But some people still like to cook over a fire. They say the food has a special taste that you cannot get from a stove.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "What does the passage suggest about cooking over a fire today?",
      "options": ["A) It is no longer possible because nobody knows how", "B) Everyone now uses a stove and never cooks over a fire", "C) Some people still choose to cook over a fire by preference", "D) People cannot boil water in a pot anymore today"],
      "answer": 2
    }
  },
  "22": {
    "description": "A1. Month 3. Hobbies and free-time activities. Frequency adverbs (always, sometimes, never, often). Simple sentences about personal preferences and habits. Vocabulary near 725 words.",
    "example": "Everyone in my family has different hobbies. I sometimes play football with my friends in the park. I often read books in the evening. I never watch TV in the morning because I do not have time. My sister always reads books on weekends. She can read for many hours. She also paints sometimes. My brother always plays computer games after school. He never plays outside. He says he does not like sport. My mother often cooks new dishes. She tries new things every week. My father sometimes plays the guitar. He is not very good, but he loves music. On Sundays, we always do something together. We often go for a walk. We sometimes visit grandparents. What do you like to do in your free time?",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "Which activity is described as something the speaker does without exception, based on the passage?",
      "options": ["A) Playing football with friends in the park", "B) Reading books in the evening at home", "C) Never watching TV in the morning", "D) Asking others what they like to do"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    "example2": "Many artists draw or paint every day. They love their work. Some artists paint with oil. Oil paint dries slowly. The colors are deep and rich. Other artists use only pencils. Pencils are simple but they can do a lot. A painter often works alone in a quiet room. The room has big windows. The light from the window is very important. The light from the window can change a picture completely. In the morning, the light is soft and yellow. In the afternoon, the light is bright and white. In the evening, the light is warm and gold. Artists always watch the light. They sometimes stop work and wait for the right light. Their patience is part of their art.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "What does the passage suggest about how artists usually work?",
      "options": ["A) All artists use the same materials in their work", "B) Light has no real effect on a painting in progress", "C) Artists choose different tools and usually work in solitude", "D) Painters always work in groups together in studios"],
      "answer": 2
    }
  },
  "23": {
    "description": "A1. Month 3–4. Describing a home — rooms, furniture, and layout. There is/are with more detail. Simple relative clauses beginning with 'that' or 'which.' Vocabulary around 775 words.",
    "example": "My house is not very big, but it is nice. There are five rooms in the house. There is a big kitchen on the first floor. The kitchen has a long table that we use for breakfast. There is also a living room next to the kitchen. The room that I like most is the living room. It has a big comfortable sofa and a small bookshelf. There is a small garden behind the house. The garden has flowers that my mother grows. Upstairs, there are two bedrooms. The bedroom that I share with my brother is small but bright. There is a window that looks at the garden. Our parents have the bigger room. The house is old, but it is full of warm memories.",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "What makes the speaker's favorite room appealing, according to the passage?",
      "options": ["A) It has a long table for breakfast each day", "B) It has a window that looks at the garden", "C) It has a comfortable sofa and a bookshelf", "D) It is the largest of the five rooms in the house"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    "example2": "Bees live in a hive. The hive is a kind of house that the bees build themselves. Inside, the hive is full of small rooms. Each room has six sides. The rooms fit together perfectly. The bees fill these rooms with honey. The honey, which they make from flowers, is their food for winter. There are also rooms for baby bees. The baby bees grow in these rooms until they are ready to fly. The queen bee, who is the largest bee in the hive, lays her eggs in special rooms. Only the queen lays eggs. The other bees, which are smaller, do all the other work. They fly out and find flowers. They bring back the sweet flower juice. The hive is a small busy city.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "What detail in the passage shows that not all rooms in the hive serve the same purpose?",
      "options": ["A) Each room has six sides that fit together", "B) The queen lays her eggs in special rooms", "C) The bees make honey from many flowers", "D) The queen is the largest bee in the hive"],
      "answer": 1
    }
  },
  "24": {
    "description": "A1. Month 4. Talking about health and body — how you feel, simple ailments. Present and past tense mixed in short narrative. Modal 'should' introduced. Vocabulary near 825 words.",
    "example": "Yesterday I did not feel well. I had a bad headache. I also felt very tired. My head hurt all morning. I did not eat lunch. I drank only water. I went to bed early in the afternoon. I slept for three hours. When I woke up, I still had the headache. My mother said I should sleep more. She said I should drink more water every day. She also said I should not look at my phone so much. My doctor told me last month that I should drink eight glasses of water every day. I should also eat more fruit. Today I feel a little better. The headache is smaller now. I will rest one more day. Then I will go back to school.",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "What does the improvement in how the speaker feels today suggest about their illness?",
      "options": ["A) They have already fully recovered from the headache", "B) The headache has not changed at all from yesterday", "C) Their condition has partially improved", "D) They need to visit the doctor again very soon"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    "example2": "The runner Anna was very fast last year. She was the best in her city. She won every race for the whole year. Her family was very proud of her. Then this year, she got sick. She had a bad cough and a high fever. She was sick for two months. She could not run. She could not even walk for long. She lost a lot of strength. Now Anna trains again every day. She runs in the park each morning. But she is not yet as fast as before. Sometimes she is sad about this. Her coach says she should be patient. He says her body needs time. He says she should not push too hard. Slowly, she is getting better.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "Why does the coach probably tell the runner to be patient, based on the passage?",
      "options": ["A) Because she has already returned to her best level", "B) Because the body needs time to recover after a long illness", "C) Because she needs to win more races very quickly now", "D) Because she should not train at all anymore"],
      "answer": 1
    }
  },
  "25": {
    "description": "A1. End of month 4. Short personal narrative about a familiar event combining present, past, and simple future. Vocabulary of about 875 words. Equivalent to a simple picture-book story or the easiest level of graded readers. Sentences have one or two clauses; content is entirely concrete and personal.",
    "example": "Last weekend I visited my grandmother. She lives in a small village two hours from my city. I took the bus on Saturday morning. The trip was long but quiet. I read my book. I watched the fields outside the window. My grandmother met me at the bus stop. Her house is small but very clean. There are flowers in every window. We cooked soup together for lunch. She showed me how to cut the vegetables. We talked about our family for many hours. She told me old stories about my mother. I felt very happy and warm. I stayed two nights at her house. On Monday morning, I took the bus home. Next time, I will bring her flowers from my garden. I love visiting her.",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "What does the speaker's plan to bring flowers next time reveal about the visit?",
      "options": ["A) They forgot to bring something this time and felt bad about it", "B) Their grandmother specifically asked for flowers as a gift", "C) The visit was positive enough that they want to do something kind in return", "D) Bringing flowers is a strict tradition in their family always"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    "example2": "Long ago, sailors traveled across the open ocean. They went on long trips for many months. They did not have maps of the open sea. They did not have phones or computers. They used the stars to find their way. At night, they looked up at the sky. They knew the patterns of the stars. The stars showed them which way to go. But the sky is not always clear. Sometimes thick clouds covered the sky for many nights. Then the sailors had a big problem. They could not see the stars. They had to guess the right way. A wrong guess could send a ship many miles off course. Some ships got lost forever. Today we have machines that show us exactly where we are.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "What does the passage suggest about sailing on cloudy nights long ago?",
      "options": ["A) It was completely safe because sailors had good paper maps", "B) It was easier than sailing on a clear and starry night", "C) It carried real risks because sailors could not see the stars", "D) Ships always returned to port to wait for clear weather"],
      "answer": 2
    }
  },
  "26": {
    "description": "A2. Month 4–5. Graded reader level 1. Short paragraphs about familiar everyday topics. Simple past narrative with time connectors (first, then, after that). Vocabulary around 1,000 words. Compound sentences with 'and,' 'but,' 'so,' 'because.'",
    "example": "Yesterday I had a busy day. First, I woke up early at six o'clock and I had breakfast. I drank coffee and ate toast. Then I walked to the bus stop, but the bus was late. I waited for ten minutes in the cold. After that, I went to work. I had three meetings in the morning. At lunch, I ate a sandwich at my desk because I did not have time to go out. In the afternoon, I worked on a report. I finished the report at four o'clock, so I could leave a little early. After work, I went to the gym for an hour. Then I came home and cooked pasta for dinner. It was a normal day, but I was very happy because it was Friday and the weekend was coming.",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "Why was the speaker happy even though it was described as a normal day?",
      "options": ["A) They woke up earlier than they usually do", "B) The bus arrived right on time at the stop", "C) It was the end of the working week and the weekend was near", "D) Breakfast was especially enjoyable that morning"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    "example2": "Plants grow slowly. The process takes many days, and each step is important. First, the seed lies under the ground for many days. The seed needs water and warmth. Then a small green stem pushes up toward the light. The stem is very thin and weak at first, but it grows stronger each day. After that, leaves open out to catch the sun. The leaves use the sunlight to make food for the plant. The plant grows taller, and the stem becomes thick. Finally, a flower forms at the top, but only if the soil is rich enough and the weather is warm enough. If the soil is poor, the plant will live, but no flower will come. So farmers care about their soil very much.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "According to the passage, what determines whether a flower will form on the plant?",
      "options": ["A) The number of days the seed spends under the ground", "B) Whether the stem grows perfectly straight upward", "C) The quality of the soil the plant is growing in", "D) How many leaves the plant manages to open up"],
      "answer": 2
    }
  },
  "27": {
    "description": "A2. Month 5. Comparing and contrasting using comparatives and superlatives. Describing differences between places, people, and things. Vocabulary near 1,100 words. Two or three sentences with comparative structures.",
    "example": "Last year I moved from a small village to a big city. The differences are huge. The city is much noisier than the village. Cars and people make sound at every hour. But the city has many more things to do. There are theaters, museums, and restaurants on every street. My new job is more interesting than my old one, and my salary is also higher. I work with smarter people. Of course, the city is more expensive too. My apartment costs three times more than my old house. The cafes are also more expensive than the ones at home. But I have to say, this is the best coffee I have ever had. The food is fresher. The shops have more choices. I am happier than before, even with the noise.",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "What trade-off does the speaker imply about living in the city?",
      "options": ["A) It is quieter but less interesting overall", "B) It is noisier and more expensive but offers much more to do", "C) It has worse coffee than the village had", "D) It is more interesting but the speaker is less happy"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    "example2": "Mountain rivers and rivers in the plains are very different. Mountain rivers are colder than rivers in the plains because the water comes from melting snow. They are also clearer because there is less dirt and mud. The fish in mountain rivers are usually smaller than the fish in big plain rivers. They grow more slowly because there is less food. Plain rivers are warmer and richer in food, so fish there grow bigger and faster. However, the small mountain fish are often considered the tastiest of all. People travel to the mountains just to catch and eat them. Restaurants charge more for mountain fish than for plain fish. Slower growth in poorer conditions seems to make the meat firmer and more flavorful.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "What trade-off does the passage suggest about mountain river fish?",
      "options": ["A) They grow more quickly because the water is colder and cleaner", "B) Their slower growth in poorer conditions appears to improve their flavor", "C) They are less tasty because there is less food in mountain rivers", "D) Plains rivers produce more fish, but mountain rivers produce no fish"],
      "answer": 1
    }
  },
  "28": {
    "description": "A2. Month 5. Talking about past experiences with 'have you ever.' Present perfect introduced for experience. Vocabulary around 1,200 words. Short personal anecdotes.",
    "example": "Have you ever visited another country? I have been to Spain twice. The first time was for a week with my family. The second time was for two weeks alone. Spain was very different from home. The food was delicious. People eat dinner very late, often at ten in the evening. The weather was much warmer than in my country. I have also been to France, but only for two days. I would like to go back. I have never been to Asia, but I want to go someday. My sister has been to Japan and she loved it. She says it is the most interesting place she has ever visited. Have you traveled a lot? Where have you been? Travel changes you in small but important ways.",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "What does the speaker's comment that Spain was 'very different from home' most likely refer to?",
      "options": ["A) Spain is much farther from home than the speaker had originally expected", "B) The food, weather, and habits of daily life felt unlike what they were used to", "C) Spain was very hard to reach and required a long and tiring journey", "D) The food in Spain was noticeably worse than the food at home"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    "example2": "Have you ever climbed a mountain? I have climbed two mountains in my life, and the experiences were very different. The first one was small and easy. I went with friends on a sunny day. We walked up in three hours. We laughed and took photos. But the second mountain nearly broke me. It was much higher. The path was steep and rocky. The weather changed many times. It rained, then it was cold, then the sun came back. I climbed for two days. My legs hurt. My back hurt. At one point, I wanted to give up, but I kept going. When I finally reached the top, I cried. I have never felt so tired and so proud at the same time. I will never forget that day.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "What does the speaker's mixed feeling about the second climb most clearly reveal?",
      "options": ["A) The second mountain was easier than the speaker had honestly expected it to be", "B) The hardest experience can also produce the strongest sense of accomplishment", "C) The speaker regrets climbing both of the mountains and would not climb again", "D) Climbing the first mountain was a more meaningful experience than the second"],
      "answer": 1
    }
  },
  "29": {
    "description": "A2. Month 5–6. Describing routines and habitual actions in the past using 'used to.' Contrasting past and present habits. Vocabulary near 1,300 words. A short descriptive paragraph about change over time.",
    "example": "When I was young, my life was very different from now. I used to play outside every day after school. I used to ride my bike for hours. I used to eat a lot of sweets, but my mother did not stop me. I used to watch cartoons on Saturday morning. I used to sleep late on weekends. Now I am thirty years old, and almost everything has changed. I work in an office from nine to five. I sit at a computer most of the day. I used to be very active, but now I have to make time for sport. I try to eat healthy food. I rarely eat sweets. I go to bed early. I used to dream of being an explorer. Now I am happy with a quieter life. People change with time.",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "What overall change does the passage suggest has occurred in the speaker's life?",
      "options": ["A) They now enjoy eating sweets even more than they did when they were young", "B) Their lifestyle has become more physically active and centered on the outdoors", "C) Their habits have shifted toward a more settled and health-conscious adult life", "D) They have come to prefer working outdoors to working in an office environment"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    "example2": "Cities used to be much smaller. A few hundred years ago, most cities were small enough to walk across in an hour or two. People used to walk everywhere because there were no cars and no buses. They used to know their neighbors. They used to shop in small markets that were always close to home. Markets used to be the center of every town. People met there to buy food, to talk, and to share news. The market was social as well as practical. Now most people drive long distances every day. Shopping happens in big stores far from where they live. People do not always know their neighbors anymore. Some things are easier now, but the daily rhythm of city life has changed in deep ways.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "What broad change in city life does the passage describe?",
      "options": ["A) Cities have grown smaller, and walking has become much more common again", "B) Daily life has moved away from a compact, walkable center toward a spread-out one", "C) Big stores have replaced cars as the main way that people travel within cities", "D) Markets remain the center of city life, though people now drive to reach them"],
      "answer": 1
    }
  },
  "30": {
    "description": "A2. Month 6. Writing simple letters and messages — informal register, greetings, and closings. Giving news and asking about someone's life. Vocabulary around 1,400 words. Friendly, conversational tone.",
    "example": "Dear Maria, how are you? I hope you and your family are well. I am fine. The weather here has been nice all week. I am sorry I have not written for a long time. I have been very busy. Last week I started a new job at a small company in the city. The work is hard and there is a lot to learn. I work long hours, sometimes until eight in the evening. But I really like my colleagues. They are kind and helpful. They invited me to lunch on my first day. How is your job going? How are the children? Please give them a kiss from me. Are you still planning to visit in the summer? I would love to see you. Please write back soon. Best wishes, Anna.",
    "exampleQuestion": {
      "question": "What is Anna's overall attitude toward her new job?",
      "options": ["A) She dislikes it because the work is hard", "B) She is neutral and not yet sure how she feels", "C) She finds it difficult but is positive about the people there", "D) She loves it completely and has no problems at all"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    "example2": "Hi Sam, thanks so much for the photos from your trip to the mountains. They are amazing. The mountains look beautiful, especially the one with the snow on top. The lake in the second photo looks so calm. I sent the photos to my sister. She wants to know exactly where you went. To be honest, I am a little jealous. I have not had a real holiday in two years. But I am also genuinely glad that you are finally taking some time off. You really deserve it after this year. The new job has been hard on you, and you have not stopped working. Take all the time you need to rest. We can plan a trip together when you come back. Talk soon, Pat.",
    "exampleQuestion2": {
      "question": "What does Pat's mention of being jealous, alongside the rest of the message, mainly express?",
      "options": ["A) Pat is genuinely upset that Sam went on the trip without including them", "B) Pat is being playful while showing real warmth and support for Sam's break", "C) Pat thinks Sam should not have taken time off after such a difficult year", "D) Pat is hinting that they expect Sam to send another invitation next time"],
      "answer": 1
    }
  },
